853019692, 1.199769069091417, 5.943506503943354, 1.2890495639294386, 6.216966019012034, 0.745316390064545, 4.351407184032723, 0.2912724460475147, 2.718837323016487], "start_times": [785973.125785438, 785973.125896578, 785973.126574024, 785973.126620777, 785975.284408993, 785976.308959822, 785976.508620833, 785976.75058413, 785977.087314738, 785977.200166948, 785978.28750225, 785979.576973895, 785983.143869821, 785983.889377325, 785985.794395302, 785986.086124446], "queue_times": [0.00013009202666580677, 0.00022792594972997904, 0.0008992969524115324, 0.0009419959969818592, 2.1587241860106587, 3.1832715740893036, 3.382929535000585, 3.624889336992055, 3.9616168179782107, 4.0744659040356055, 5.161797680077143, 6.451265782932751, 10.018158765975386, 10.763662935933098, 12.668677847948857, 12.960403681965545], "generated_texts": ["", "Below is a **complete, minimal, and ready\u2011to\u2011run** Rust application that\n\n1. Reads the current clipboard text (Windows, macOS, Linux).\n2. Sends that text to the ElevenLabs Text\u2011to\u2011Speech API.\n3. Streams the returned MP3/OGG audio straight to your speakers.\n\n> **Why this works out\u2011of\u2011the\u2011box**  \n> * `arboard` \u2013 cross\u2011platform clipboard access.  \n> * `reqwest` \u2013 async HTTP client (the ElevenLabs API is HTTPS).  \n> * `serde` \u2013 JSON (de)serialization.  \n> * `rodio` \u2013 simple audio playback that can consume a byte stream.  \n> * `tokio` \u2013 async runtime required by `reqwest`.  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Prerequisites\n\n| Item | How to get it |\n|------|---------------|\n| **Rust** | `curl https://sh.rustup.rs -sSf | sh` (or your package manager) |\n| **ElevenLabs API key** | Sign up at <https://elevenlabs.io>, go to *API* \u2192 *API Key*. |\n| **Voice ID** | In the ElevenLabs dashboard, pick a voice and copy its ID (e.g. `EXAMPLE-VOICE-ID`). |\n\n> **Tip** \u2013 Keep the API key secret. Store it in an environment variable or a `.env` file.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project layout\n\n```\nelevenlabs-tts/\n\u251c\u2500 Cargo.toml\n\u2514\u2500 src/\n   \u2514\u2500 main.rs\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cargo.toml\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"elevenlabs-tts\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[dependencies]\ntokio = { version = \"1\", features = [\"full\"] }\nreqwest = { version = \"0.12\", features = [\"json\", \"stream\"] }\nserde = { version = \"1\", features = [\"derive\"] }\nserde_json = \"1\"\narboard = \"3\"\nrodio = \"0.17\"\ndotenvy = \"0.", "Sure thing! To make the \u201cFilters\u201d section clearer and easier to read, I\u2019ll need the current version of the document (or at least the part that contains the Filters). Once I have that, I can rewrite it in a more", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "## LCD\u2011Control System with a Potentiometer and a Temperature Sensor  \n*(Arduino\u2011based, using an LCD\u202fI\u00b2C backpack and either an LM35 or a DS18B20)*  \n\nBelow is a complete, step\u2011by\u2011step guide that shows you how to:\n\n1. **Read a temperature sensor** (LM35 or DS18B20).  \n2. **Read a potentiometer** to control a parameter (e.g., LCD back\u2011light brightness or a temperature threshold).  \n3. **Display the data on a 16\u00d72 LCD** that is driven through an I\u00b2C backpack.  \n4. **Use the potentiometer to adjust the LCD back\u2011light** (or any other variable you wish).  \n\nFeel free to mix and match the parts \u2013 the wiring and code are almost identical for both sensors, except for the way the temperature is read.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Parts & Wiring\n\n| Component | Pin | Arduino Pin | Notes |\n|-----------|-----|-------------|-------|\n| **LM35** | VCC | 5\u202fV | Power |\n| | GND | GND | Ground |\n| |", "", "### Quick answer\n\n```js\n", "", ""], "errors": ["", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""], "max_output_tokens_per_s": 116.0, "max_concurrent_requests": 8, "rtfx": 0.0, "spec_decode_acceptance_rate": 34.59037711313394, "spec_decode_acceptance_length": 2.3836150845253576, "spec_decod